This document illustrates a chemical reaction pathway for the **reduction of carbon dioxide (CO2) to carbon monoxide (CO)**, mediated by a rhenium (Re) bipyridine carbonyl chloride complex.

The process is depicted in two main steps:
1.  **Charge Transfer (CT):** The initial neutral rhenium complex, [Re(bipy)(CO)3Cl], undergoes a one-electron reduction to form a radical anion, [Re(bipy)(CO)3Cl]•-.
2.  **CO2 Activation and Reduction:** This reduced radical anion then reacts with carbon dioxide (CO2). In this step, CO2 is converted into carbon monoxide (CO). Part of this newly formed CO becomes a ligand on the rhenium center, increasing the carbonyl count from three to four ([Re(bipy)(CO)4Cl]), and another part is released as a free CO molecule.

In essence, the diagram shows a catalytic or stoichiometric conversion of CO2 into CO, utilizing a rhenium complex as the active species after an initial electron transfer.
